[AIMessage → tool] create_analysis({'text': 'Based on non-Wikipedia sources 
identified so far, the "Ring of Nestor" can be characterized for the article as 
a gold signet ring attributed to the Late Bronze Age Aegean world. Scholarly 
literature distinguishes two main traditions relevant to the name: (1) a gold 
ring from a burial at Thisbe in Boeotia, acquired by Arthur Evans and later held
in the Ashmolean Museum, whose elaborate iconography has led to associations 
with Minoan or early Mycenaean religious imagery and whose authenticity has been
debated; and (2) a gold signet ring from the "Griffin Warrior" tomb near the 
palace of Nestor at Pylos, which has sometimes been referred to in popular media
as connected with Nestor but is better treated as a separate object. For a 
focused encyclopedic article, the primary subject will be the Ashmolean ring 
commonly discussed in scholarship as the "Ring of Nestor", while the Pylos 
discoveries will be mentioned in passing to avoid confusion. The article will 
emphasize discovery context, physical description and iconography, dating and 
cultural attribution, interpretations of symbolism and function, and provenance 
and authenticity debates, all with explicit citations and a non-promotional tone
in line with Wikipedia\'s neutrality and words-to-watch guidance.', 'query_id': 
None})
