submit_final_report({'title': 'Ring of Nestor', 'sections': [{'title': '', 
'section_type': 'lead', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': [], 'blocks': [{'text': 'The 
Ring of Nestor is a gold signet ring that has become one of the most discussed 
artifacts of Aegean archaeology, noted for its elaborate engraved scene and its 
role in debates over Minoan and Mycenaean religious imagery, cultural contacts, 
and issues of authenticity.[4][16][17][18][19] Originally published by Arthur 
Evans as part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead-seals from 
a burial at Thisbê in Boeotia, the ring has since been linked in scholarship 
with the wider Mycenaean world, including the region of Pylos and the Palace of 
Nestor.[4][16][17] Its complex iconography, often interpreted as depicting a 
vision of the afterlife or a ritual scene, has made it a key reference point in 
discussions of Aegean religion and elite funerary symbolism.[1][4][16][18][19]',
'source_ids': ['20260628170909519788_a01c45aa', '20260628171339806409_fa8217b3',
