consistently described in scholarship as a gold signet-type ring, part of a 
broader assemblage of gold signet rings and bead-seals associated with elite 
burial contexts in central Greece.[4][16][18] Evans noted that the surface of 
the ring was covered by a dark film interpreted as a decorative layer, 
highlighting both its precious material and the care taken in its 
finishing.[16][18]', 'source_ids': ['20260628170909519788_a01c45aa', 
'20260628171339806409_fa8217b3', '20260628171339908709_309f4c44'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'The engraved bezel of the ring carries a complex figural
scene incorporating a tree, tendril-like or ivy-like vegetation, human figures, 
and what are described as supernatural or otherworldly motifs, which together 
form a dense iconographic field.[1][4][16][18][19] Comparisons with gold rings 
from the Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos emphasise the close similarity of details
such as the woody ivy growing from a tree, which has been used as a key argument
for the ring’s stylistic coherence with securely excavated Minoan or Mycenaean 
