[{'text': 'Arthur Evans first brought the Ring of Nestor to scholarly attention 
in a study of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead-seals said to 
come from a burial at Thisbê in Boeotia, central Greece.[4][16] Evans described 
the assemblage as a funerary treasure, using it to argue for the presence and 
influence of Minoan religious and artistic traditions on the Greek 
mainland.[4][16]', 'source_ids': ['20260628170909519788_a01c45aa', 
'20260628171339806409_fa8217b3'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Evans stated 
