'20260628171027155279_8110edf0', '20260628171027244534_70a2384b'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Dating and cultural attribution', 'section_type': 
'body', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': [], 'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor is
generally placed within the broader horizon of Late Bronze Age Aegean gold 
signet rings, whose floruit spans the later Middle and early Late Bronze Age, 
and it is treated as part of an early Mycenaean corpus that shows strong 
stylistic and iconographic ties to Minoan Crete.[4][16][18] Studies of gold 
rings and sealstones from the Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor, 
dated to around 1500 BC and assigned to the Late Helladic IIA phase, emphasise 
that their imagery and craftsmanship are closely linked to Cretan iconography, 
and the Ring of Nestor is repeatedly compared to this securely dated 
material.[11][12][14][15][18][19]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628170909519788_a01c45aa', '20260628171339806409_fa8217b3', 
'20260628171339908709_309f4c44', '20260628171027119891_de1e357b', 
'20260628171027155279_8110edf0', '20260628171027210375_bc2e325e', 
'20260628171027244534_70a2384b', '20260628171339938421_ee224af1'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Within this framework, the ring is variously described 
as Minoan, Minoan-style, or Mycenaean with strong Minoan influence, reflecting 
the broader debate over the extent of Cretan artistic and religious impact on 
early Mycenaean elites.[4][16][18][19] Iconographic analyses emphasise that the 
ring’s imagery fits within the repertoire of Minoan religious scenes known from 
Cretan sealstones and rings, while its reported mainland funerary context links 
it to Mycenaean burial practices.[1][4][16][18]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628170909519788_a01c45aa', '20260628171339806409_fa8217b3', 
'20260628171339908709_309f4c44', '20260628171339938421_ee224af1', 
'20260628170909385983_6206121d'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Iconography
