'blocks': [{'text': 'Scholars have long treated the Ring of Nestor as a key 
piece of evidence for reconstructing aspects of Minoan and related Aegean 
religious belief, particularly ideas about the afterlife.[1][4][16] Evans framed
the ring as offering a \x1cglimpse into the Minoan after-world\x1d, arguing that
its complex scene represented a vision of the dead and their journey or status 
in the next world.[4][16]', 'source_ids': ['20260628170909519788_a01c45aa', 
'20260628171339806409_fa8217b3', '20260628170909385983_6206121d'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Later iconographic studies emphasise specific elements 
of the composition, such as the tree with ivy-like growths, the arrangement of 
human figures, and associated supernatural motifs, interpreting them as part of 
a ritual or cultic scene that might involve offerings, epiphanies of deities, or
symbolic representations of the soul’s transition.[1][16][18][19] These studies 
situate the ring within a wider corpus of Minoan and Mycenaean gold rings and 
sealstones whose imagery is read as depicting ceremonial or mythological 
narratives.[18][19]', 'source_ids': ['20260628170909385983_6206121d', 
'20260628171339806409_fa8217b3', '20260628171339908709_309f4c44', 
'20260628171339938421_ee224af1'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Some 
interpretations relate the ring more specifically to elite funerary ideology, 
suggesting that the scene articulates the privileged status of high-ranking 
individuals in the afterlife and the centrality of ritual in securing that 
status.[4][16][18][19] In this view, the Ring of Nestor is considered an emblem 
of aristocratic identity, part of a broader set of gold rings that signal both 
the wealth of their owners and their participation in a shared religious imagery
spanning Crete and the Greek mainland.[11][12][14][18][19]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628170909519788_a01c45aa', '20260628171339806409_fa8217b3', 
'20260628171339908709_309f4c44', '20260628171339938421_ee224af1', 
'20260628171027119891_de1e357b', '20260628171027155279_8110edf0', 
'20260628171027210375_bc2e325e'], 'kind': 'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Function 
and significance', 'section_type': 'body', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': [], 
'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor is typically classified as a signet 
ring, implying a functional role in sealing and authentication, but its 
elaborate imagery and association with a sepulchral treasure underline its 
symbolic and ceremonial importance.[4][16][18] Discussions of the Griffin 
Warrior tomb rings, which are closely related in style and iconography, stress 
that such gold signet rings served as markers of status and as vehicles for 
religious or ideological messages, rather than as everyday utilitarian 
objects.[11][12][14][15][18][19]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628170909519788_a01c45aa', '20260628171339806409_fa8217b3', 
'20260628171339908709_309f4c44', '20260628171027119891_de1e357b', 
