'20260628171027244534_70a2384b', '20260628171339938421_ee224af1'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Because the ring is tied in scholarship to both the 
Boeotian sepulchral treasure and the Pylian elite milieu around the Palace of 
Nestor, it has become a focal point in discussions of long-distance connections,
cultural exchange, and the formation of early Mycenaean 
ideology.[4][11][12][14][15][17][18][19] Its imagery and material provide a case
study for how Minoan religious and artistic motifs were adopted, adapted, or 
re-signified by mainland elites in the context of funerary display and political
self-presentation.[1][4][16][18][19]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628170909519788_a01c45aa', '20260628171027119891_de1e357b', 
'20260628171027155279_8110edf0', '20260628171027210375_bc2e325e', 
