[], 'blocks': [{'text': 'A study of the Ring of Nestor notes that in 1995 the 
ring was once again placed on public display in the Ashmolean Museum in Oxford, 
indicating that by the late twentieth century it formed part of the Ashmolean’s 
collections.[2][3] The same study situates the ring within broader discussions 
of Aegean forgeries, underscoring that its museum history is intertwined with 
debates over authenticity.[2][3]', 'source_ids': 
['20260628170909428426_f674491d', '20260628170909475925_83fd40b1'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}]}, {'title': 'Authenticity and scholarly debate', 'section_type': 
'conflict_disclosure', 'disclosed_conflict_ids': 
['20260628170931694332_8e515b10'], 'blocks': [{'text': 'The Ring of Nestor has 
been at the centre of a long-running debate over the authenticity of certain 
Aegean antiquities.[1][2][3] Studies on forged or questionable Aegean artifacts 
frequently use the ring as a key case, noting that its lack of secure excavation
context and its acquisition through the antiquities market raised suspicions 
that it might be a modern or \x1cpseudo-Minoan\x1d creation.[1][2][3][9]', 
'source_ids': ['20260628170909385983_6206121d', '20260628170909428426_f674491d',
'20260628170909475925_83fd40b1', '20260628170927305731_5fb47c23'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'One strand of scholarship, including the initial 
interpretations by Evans and later iconographic analyses, has treated the ring 
as an authentic ancient artifact, emphasising its close stylistic and 
iconographic parallels with securely excavated Minoan and Mycenaean gold rings 
and its integration into a coherent repertoire of Aegean religious 
imagery.[4][16][18][19] For these scholars, newly discovered rings such as those
from the Griffin Warrior tomb, whose woody ivy and other details closely match 
those on the Ring of Nestor, provide positive evidence supporting the ring’s 
authenticity.[17][18][19]', 'source_ids': ['20260628170909519788_a01c45aa', 
'20260628171339806409_fa8217b3', '20260628171339908709_309f4c44', 
'20260628171339938421_ee224af1', '20260628171339876710_e202618c'], 'kind': 
'paragraph'}, {'text': 'Another line of argument, exemplified by a study 
explicitly labelling the object the \x1cPseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring\x1d, questions
its status as a genuine Minoan or Mycenaean work, highlighting instead its 
Egyptian iconographic sources and treating it as a problematic or composite 
piece within the corpus of Aegean art.[9] This critical perspective places the 
ring within the broader phenomenon of \x1cpseudo-Minoan\x1d objects and stresses
the need for caution in using it as straightforward evidence for Aegean 
religious or artistic traditions.[1][2][3][9]', 'source_ids': 
